To create a new line within a cell in a LaTeX table, you can use the `\\` command. This command is used to start a new line within the same cell. Here’s a simple example to illustrate how to do it:

```latex
\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|c|}
\hline
This is a cell with a \\ new line. \\ This line continues here. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Example of a table with a new line in a cell}
\end{table}
```

In this example, the `\\` command is used to create a new line within the cell. The `|c|` in the `tabular` environment specifies that the table has a single column centered. The `\hline` commands are used to draw horizontal lines above and below the cell.

If you need more complex formatting, you can use the `multirow` package, which allows for more advanced cell spanning and line breaking within cells. Here’s an example using `multirow`:

```latex
\usepackage{multirow}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|c|}
\hline
\multirow{2}{*}{This is a cell with a \\ new line. \\ This line continues here.} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Example of a table with a new line in a cell using multirow}
\end{table}
